## Complete Mockito Example

```java
@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)
class OrderServiceTest {

    @Mock
    private OrderRepository orderRepo;
    @Mock
    private PaymentGateway paymentGateway;
    @Mock
    private ApplicationEventPublisher eventPublisher;

    @InjectMocks
    private OrderService orderService;

    @Test
    void shouldCreateOrderWithPayment() {
        // ARRANGE: stub collaborators
        Order savedOrder = Order.builder().id(1L).status(PENDING).build();
        when(orderRepo.save(any(Order.class))).thenReturn(savedOrder);
        when(paymentGateway.charge(any(), any())).thenReturn(PaymentResult.success("tx-123"));

        // ACT
        Order result = orderService.createOrder(buildRequest());

        // ASSERT: return value
        assertThat(result.getId()).isEqualTo(1L);

        // ASSERT: verify interactions
        verify(orderRepo).save(any(Order.class));
        verify(paymentGateway).charge(
            eq(BigDecimal.valueOf(99.99)),  // Exact amount
            any(PaymentCard.class)
        );
        verify(eventPublisher).publishEvent(any(OrderCreatedEvent.class));
    }

    @Test
    void shouldNotPublishEventWhenPaymentFails() {
        when(paymentGateway.charge(any(), any())).thenReturn(PaymentResult.failure("DECLINED"));

        assertThatThrownBy(() -> orderService.createOrder(buildRequest()))
            .isInstanceOf(PaymentDeclinedException.class);

        // Verify event was NOT published:
        verifyNoInteractions(eventPublisher);
    }
}
```

## ArgumentCaptor — Capture for Assertion

```java
@Test
void shouldSendEmailWithCorrectContent() {
    // Setup
    Order order = buildOrder(42L, "user@example.com");
    when(orderRepo.save(any())).thenReturn(order);

    // Act
    orderService.createOrder(buildRequest());

    // Capture the Email object sent to emailService:
    ArgumentCaptor<Email> emailCaptor = ArgumentCaptor.forClass(Email.class);
    verify(emailService).send(emailCaptor.capture());

    // Assert on the captured argument:
    Email capturedEmail = emailCaptor.getValue();
    assertThat(capturedEmail.getTo()).isEqualTo("user@example.com");
    assertThat(capturedEmail.getSubject()).contains("Order #42");
    assertThat(capturedEmail.getBody()).contains("confirmed");
}
```

## Stubbing Behaviors

```java
// Return different values on successive calls:
when(orderRepo.findById(1L))
    .thenReturn(Optional.empty())     // First call
    .thenReturn(Optional.of(order)); // Second call

// Throw exception:
when(orderRepo.findById(99L))
    .thenThrow(new DataAccessException("DB error") {});

// Throw on specific call count:
when(orderRepo.findById(1L))
    .thenReturn(Optional.of(order))
    .thenThrow(new RuntimeException("Unexpected second call"));

// Do nothing for void methods:
doNothing().when(emailService).send(any(Email.class));

// Throw for void methods:
doThrow(new EmailException("SMTP error"))
    .when(emailService).send(any(Email.class));

// Capture return value from void method:
doAnswer(invocation -> {
    Email email = invocation.getArgument(0);
    log.info("Mock email sent to: {}", email.getTo());
    return null;
}).when(emailService).send(any(Email.class));
```

## @Spy — Partial Mock

```java
@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)
class PricingServiceTest {

    @Spy
    private PricingService pricingService;  // Real instance — not a mock!

    @Test
    void shouldApplyDiscountCorrectly() {
        // Stub ONE method — rest use real implementation:
        doReturn(new BigDecimal("0.15"))
            .when(pricingService).getDiscountRate("PREMIUM");  // Stub this

        // Real calculateTotal() will be called:
        BigDecimal price = pricingService.calculateFinalPrice(product, "PREMIUM");
        // calculateFinalPrice() is real code; getDiscountRate() is stubbed

        assertThat(price).isEqualByComparingTo("84.99");
    }
}
```

## Verification Matchers

```java
// Exact count:
verify(emailService, times(2)).send(any());  // Called exactly twice
verify(emailService, never()).send(any());   // Never called
verify(emailService, atLeast(1)).send(any());
verify(emailService, atMost(3)).send(any());

// Argument matchers:
verify(emailService).send(argThat(email ->
    email.getTo().equals("user@example.com") &&
    email.getSubject().contains("Order")
));

// In order:
InOrder inOrder = inOrder(orderRepo, emailService);
inOrder.verify(orderRepo).save(any(Order.class));    // Must happen first
inOrder.verify(emailService).send(any(Email.class)); // Then this

// No more interactions after verified ones:
verify(emailService).send(any());
verifyNoMoreInteractions(emailService);  // Fail if any other calls
```

## Production Reality

Mix of `when()/thenReturn()` for queries and `verify()` for commands (write operations). For query methods that return values, assert on the result — don't also verify the call was made (redundant). For command methods that have side effects (save, publish, send), verify they were called. This avoids over-specifying which reduces test brittleness.

## @Mock — Pure Unit Test (Fast)

```java
@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)  // No Spring — pure Mockito
class OrderServiceUnitTest {

    @Mock
    private OrderRepository orderRepo;  // Mockito mock — no Spring bean
    @Mock
    private EmailService emailService;

    @InjectMocks
    private OrderService orderService;  // OrderService created by Mockito, mocks injected

    @Test
    void shouldCreateOrder() {
        when(orderRepo.save(any())).thenReturn(buildOrder(1L));

        Order result = orderService.createOrder(buildRequest());

        assertThat(result.getId()).isEqualTo(1L);
    }
    // Test runs in ~10ms — no Spring context
}
```

## @MockBean — Spring Context Test

```java
@WebMvcTest(OrderController.class)  // Loads only web layer beans
class OrderControllerTest {

    @Autowired
    private MockMvc mockMvc;  // Spring-managed MockMvc

    @MockBean  // Replace OrderService bean in Spring context with mock
    private OrderService orderService;

    @MockBean  // Replace OrderMapper bean
    private OrderMapper orderMapper;

    @Test
    void shouldReturnOrderWhenFound() throws Exception {
        Order order = buildOrder(42L);
        OrderResponse response = buildOrderResponse(42L);

        when(orderService.findById(42L)).thenReturn(order);
        when(orderMapper.toResponse(order)).thenReturn(response);

        mockMvc.perform(get("/api/orders/42")
                .contentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON))
            .andExpect(status().isOk())
            .andExpect(jsonPath("$.id").value(42));
    }
    // Test takes ~2-5 seconds — Spring context needs to start
}
```

## Performance Comparison

```
Test type         | Annotation     | Context | Speed
------------------|----------------|---------|--------
Unit (service)    | @Mock          | None    | ~10ms
Controller slice  | @MockBean      | Partial | ~2-5s
Integration test  | @MockBean      | Full    | ~10-30s
End-to-end        | None           | Full+DB | ~30-60s

Build with 100 tests:
  All unit tests: 100 × 10ms = 1 second
  All @WebMvcTest: 100 × 3s = 5 minutes (context reused across tests in same class)
  All @SpringBootTest: 100 × 20s = 33 minutes

Solution: use @DirtiesContext sparingly,
         group @WebMvcTest classes for context reuse,
         use @MockBean only when Spring wiring is being tested
```

## Context Reuse

```java
// Spring caches application context — same config = reuse
@WebMvcTest(OrderController.class)
class OrderControllerTest { ... }  // Starts context

@WebMvcTest(OrderController.class)
class OrderControllerSecurityTest { ... }  // REUSES same context — fast!

// But:
@WebMvcTest(OrderController.class)
class OrderControllerTest {
    @MockBean
    private OrderService orderService;  // Different @MockBean set = different context!
}

@WebMvcTest(OrderController.class)
class OrderControllerTest2 {
    @MockBean
    private OrderService orderService;
    @MockBean
    private AuditService auditService;  // Extra @MockBean = NEW context
}

// Tip: consolidate controller tests in one class to share context
```

## Production Reality

Prefer `@Mock` + `@InjectMocks` for service/domain unit tests — they're fast and focused. Reserve `@MockBean` + `@WebMvcTest` for controller tests that verify HTTP behavior: status codes, request mapping, JSON serialization, validation errors. Don't use `@SpringBootTest` unless you're testing the full stack — it's the slowest and should be the smallest category of your test suite.
